1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
He created and flew the fastest plane in the world.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
He was the driving force behind the largest aircraft ever made.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
His engineers pioneered innovative designs for missiles,

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:15,000
sent the first communications satellites aloft,

5
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:19,000
and developed top secret projects for the U.S. military.

6
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:24,000
A romantic figure in his youth, he ended an enigma and a recluse,

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,000
and one of America's most successful entrepreneurs.

8
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:30,000
Now, Howard Hughes Tech on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Howard Hughes changed the world.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
His high-speed aircraft were prototypes for future fighter planes.

11
00:00:54,001 --> 00:00:55,800
fighter planes.

12
00:00:55,900 --> 00:00:59,240
He jump-started commercial aviation with airplanes that

13
00:00:59,340 --> 00:01:03,600
flew higher and faster than the competition.

14
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:07,540
His big dreams required new aircraft control systems still

15
00:01:07,640 --> 00:01:09,540
in use today.

16
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:12,160
His engineers made the first remote control missiles a

17
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:16,460
reality and made live TV possible around the globe.

18
00:01:20,100 --> 00:01:24,000
Christmas Eve, 1905.

19
00:01:24,001 --> 00:01:26,260
Howard Robard Hughes, Jr. was born.

20
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:30,500
But as in much of his life, even his birth date can be questioned.

21
00:01:31,300 --> 00:01:33,380
There was no official birth certificate on file.

22
00:01:34,180 --> 00:01:38,740
What is known is that his parents fostered young Howard's interest in all things mechanical.

23
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:45,420
As a child, he took everything apart so that he could put it back together again.

24
00:01:45,421 --> 00:01:49,260
He took a bicycle and modified it with a motor.

25
00:01:49,261 --> 00:01:57,120
And his mother called the local newspaper, and Howard was photographed holding the first motorized bicycle in Houston.

26
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:02,740
Hughes got his pilot's license in 1928 and bought himself a biplane.

27
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:06,000
He could well afford to indulge in expensive toys.

28
00:02:07,320 --> 00:02:12,840
His father was the developer of a specialized oil drilling bit that made the Hughes family wealthy.

29
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:17,540
The Hughes drill bit revolutionized oil drilling.

30
00:02:17,541 --> 00:02:22,160
and that immediately made Hughes Tool Company an extremely valuable property.

31
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:26,220
So Hughes early on saw the power that inventions can have

32
00:02:26,221 --> 00:02:29,740
in changing our way of life and in making certainly a lot of money.

33
00:02:30,940 --> 00:02:33,660
Young Howard, anxious to make his mark in the world,

34
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:38,120
left Houston before completing college and moved to Los Angeles to make movies.

35
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:42,040
In 1930, he made a name for himself as a film director

36
00:02:42,041 --> 00:02:46,000
with Hell's Angels, a World War I aerial epic.

37
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:50,200
He would later make The Outlaw, starring Jane Russell.

38
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:55,220
Hollywood legend has it that, using his knowledge of aerodynamics,

39
00:02:55,780 --> 00:02:59,680
he designed an underwire brawl that helped her stand out as an actress.

40
00:03:00,400 --> 00:03:03,080
Apparently it was so uncomfortable she never wore it in the movie.

41
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:09,340
Hughes continued to make movies, but he also turned his focus to flying and designing aircraft.

42
00:03:10,740 --> 00:03:16,340
By the summer of 1935, Hughes decided he wanted to set a new aircraft speed record.

43
00:03:17,540 --> 00:03:21,780
He hired two men to help make his dream come true.

44
00:03:23,340 --> 00:03:25,680
He had a fellow by the name of Glenn Oedekirk,

45
00:03:25,820 --> 00:03:29,020
who was working on his airplanes, who became an expert mechanic,

46
00:03:29,021 --> 00:03:31,680
and he hired a fellow by the name of Dick Palmer,

47
00:03:32,160 --> 00:03:35,420
who was probably one of the best airplane designers of that time.

48
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:38,980
In the capable hands of Oedekirk and Palmer,

49
00:03:39,700 --> 00:03:43,440
Hugh's dream took shape as a plane he called the H-1.

50
00:03:44,520 --> 00:03:47,520
They referred to this as an engine with a saddle on it.

51
00:03:47,540 --> 00:03:52,380
it because the cockpit is very small and there's just not much wiggle room inside.

52
00:03:52,720 --> 00:03:56,040
But it was designed for high speed and that's what he got.

53
00:03:57,420 --> 00:04:03,720
Like other fast, small planes, it was a single-seater with an open cockpit and a lot of horsepower.

54
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:09,200
But Hugh's quest for speed took the H-1 into uncharted design territory.

55
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:16,480
There was a bell-shaped engine cowling to reduce airframe drag, a wing curve that stabilized

56
00:04:16,481 --> 00:04:21,240
air flow and landing gear that retracted into the fuselage.

57
00:04:22,340 --> 00:04:25,020
The streamlining continued with a new technique.

58
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:29,920
Rivets and joints were fabricated flush against the aircraft's smooth aluminum skin.

59
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:32,360
But it was his airplane.

60
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:34,380
He supervised every bit of the design.

61
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:40,480
That Hughes played such an important part in the conceptual design of the H-1 was remarkable.

62
00:04:42,100 --> 00:04:44,340
He had no formal training in engineering,

63
00:04:44,341 --> 00:04:47,180
but he had an unquenchable thirst for knowledge.

64
00:04:48,320 --> 00:04:52,480
Howard Hughes was one of the world's great brain pickers

65
00:04:52,481 --> 00:04:56,540
and he would sit with the engineers who were working on designs

66
00:04:56,541 --> 00:04:59,520
and how do you solve this and how do you solve that problem

67
00:04:59,521 --> 00:05:02,060
and what do you do for this and what do you do for that.

68
00:05:03,020 --> 00:05:05,940
He literally picked their brains of the things they were doing

69
00:05:05,941 --> 00:05:07,180
and knew what they were doing.

70
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:09,040
He was a very smart guy

71
00:05:09,041 --> 00:05:13,540
and he certainly had certain interests in mathematics and science.

72
00:05:13,541 --> 00:05:19,720
He was fascinated by how engines work, not only how you can make them go faster,

73
00:05:19,960 --> 00:05:22,720
but he was interested in electronics in general.

74
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:29,200
Hugh's new invention, the H-1, was ready on September 13, 1935.

75
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:32,600
Serving as his own test pilot in the racer,

76
00:05:33,480 --> 00:05:37,780
he set a new world speed record of 352 miles per hour,

77
00:05:38,340 --> 00:05:41,460
passing the existing mark of 314 miles per hour.

78
00:05:41,461 --> 00:05:44,760
He was so happy with the results of the speedy plane

79
00:05:44,860 --> 00:05:47,800
that he kept it aloft longer than he should have.

80
00:05:47,900 --> 00:05:52,600
True to Hugh's fashion, he kept flying pass after pass after

81
00:05:52,700 --> 00:05:55,500
pass, hoping that he could end up with a higher speed,

82
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:59,220
and eventually ran out of gas, landed gear up in a beat field,

83
00:05:59,320 --> 00:06:01,720
damaged the airplane, didn't hurt himself.

84
00:06:01,820 --> 00:06:04,320
The first thing he told to Oda Kirk when he came out

85
00:06:04,420 --> 00:06:06,120
is, she'll fly faster, Odie.

86
00:06:06,220 --> 00:06:07,220
She'll fly faster.

87
00:06:07,320 --> 00:06:09,520
I know I can do 365.

88
00:06:09,521 --> 00:06:13,440
I looked about and decided this field was the best spot.

89
00:06:13,960 --> 00:06:17,520
Put the plane down here with the retractable landing gear retracted

90
00:06:17,521 --> 00:06:21,320
as landings in rough country are safer with the wheels up.

91
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:26,500
Hughes went on to break the transcontinental record with the H-1 racer,

92
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:30,480
flying at high altitude and breathing compressed oxygen in the cabin.

93
00:06:31,820 --> 00:06:33,900
The thin air made for greater speeds,

94
00:06:34,220 --> 00:06:35,960
and the trip from Burbank to Newark

95
00:06:35,961 --> 00:06:39,660
took just 7 hours, 28 minutes, and 25 seconds.

96
00:06:42,680 --> 00:06:46,220
Next, Hughes wanted to fly around the world faster than anyone ever had,

97
00:06:47,140 --> 00:06:53,800
and in the process beat Charles Lindbergh's 33-and-a-half-hour record time to Paris, set in 1927.

98
00:06:54,900 --> 00:07:00,500
But in technological terms, Hughes' journey would vastly differ from Lindbergh's.

99
00:07:00,501 --> 00:07:03,940
The aircraft Hughes chose was the Lockheed 14,

100
00:07:03,941 --> 00:07:06,800
a twin-engine 12-passenger airplane.

101
00:07:06,801 --> 00:07:10,240
It would need significant modification

102
00:07:10,241 --> 00:07:13,120
to make it worthy of the proposed around-the-world flight.

103
00:07:13,121 --> 00:07:18,420
The Lockheed was fitted with two specially supercharged

104
00:07:18,421 --> 00:07:20,780
Wright G-102 Cyclone engines.

105
00:07:20,781 --> 00:07:25,120
And Hughes spared no expense to outfit the plane

106
00:07:25,121 --> 00:07:26,760
with the latest navigation equipment.

107
00:07:26,761 --> 00:07:28,960
If you go back to Lindbergh, I mean,

108
00:07:28,961 --> 00:07:35,040
Lindbergh was one fellow in a small cockpit with a handful of gauges flying across the Atlantic.

109
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:41,320
The cockpit of the Lockheed 14 was absolutely jammed with instruments of every conceivable kind.

110
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:45,020
He was one of this, not just to be a triumph of courage,

111
00:07:45,580 --> 00:07:48,520
which is what everybody thought of Lindbergh, and rightly so.

112
00:07:48,840 --> 00:07:51,220
He wanted it to be very much a triumph of science.

113
00:07:52,860 --> 00:07:55,880
Resourceful Aviator also planned on having help from the ground.

114
00:07:55,881 --> 00:08:02,880
He had set up communications with all the ham operators around the world so they could track him where he was, read noise location.

115
00:08:07,880 --> 00:08:13,880
On July 10, 1938, the weather was good and Hughes said the Lockheed was ready to go.

116
00:08:13,881 --> 00:08:16,302
was ready to go.

117
00:08:16,303 --> 00:08:27,200
The aircraft was so heavy with fuel that Hughes almost ran out of runway

118
00:08:27,201 --> 00:08:41,201
before he was airborne. But the speed of Hughes' journey astonished the world.

119
00:08:41,577 --> 00:08:43,420
He and his

120
00:08:43,421 --> 00:08:47,800
crew of four reached Paris in 16 hours and 38 minutes,

121
00:08:47,801 --> 00:08:50,080
cutting Lindbergh's time in half.

122
00:08:50,081 --> 00:08:52,600
After another 7 hour and 51 minute flight,

123
00:08:52,601 --> 00:08:54,660
Hughes reached Moscow.

124
00:08:54,661 --> 00:08:57,460
A 30 hour and 18 minute series of flights after that,

125
00:08:57,461 --> 00:09:00,360
they landed in Fairbanks, Alaska.

126
00:09:00,361 --> 00:09:03,760
Then, after another 16 hour and 27 minute flight,

127
00:09:03,761 --> 00:09:08,720
at 2.37 PM on July 14, 1938, Hughes completed

128
00:09:08,721 --> 00:09:11,960
the last part of the trip from Fairbanks to Minneapolis

129
00:09:11,961 --> 00:09:13,200
to New York.

130
00:09:13,201 --> 00:09:18,060
The entire trip had taken just three days, 19 hours, and 17 minutes.

131
00:09:24,180 --> 00:09:32,220
The following day, more than a million people lined the sidewalks of New York City for a ticker tape parade for their hero, who had circled the globe.

132
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:38,140
He wanted to bring attention to himself as an aviation pioneer, a scientific pioneer.

133
00:09:38,141 --> 00:09:46,693
And he was also, he wanted to see exactly how much of the new technology at the time could be put to work in a way that was safe and would show people the potential

134
00:09:46,694 --> 00:09:47,840
wonders of air travel.

135
00:09:49,820 --> 00:09:55,500
After developing aviation technology, Hughes felt he was ready to develop his aviation business.

136
00:09:56,920 --> 00:10:04,180
In May 1939, he began buying stock in an airline that would become known by its initials, TWA.

137
00:10:04,181 --> 00:10:08,180
Hughes knew just the plane he wanted TWA to fly,

138
00:10:08,181 --> 00:10:11,180
the Lockheed Constellation.

139
00:10:11,181 --> 00:10:15,180
He set the design spec high, demanding powerful engines.

140
00:10:15,181 --> 00:10:19,180
The Constellation was a technologically refined aircraft.

141
00:10:19,181 --> 00:10:21,180
It had a high power-to-weight ratio,

142
00:10:21,181 --> 00:10:25,180
and it developed a lot of thrust for a comparatively small

143
00:10:25,181 --> 00:10:26,180
airframe.

144
00:10:26,181 --> 00:10:29,180
3350 engines allowed the Constellation to cruise comfortably

145
00:10:29,181 --> 00:10:33,180
at 330 miles an hour, you know, faster than a lot of the

146
00:10:33,181 --> 00:10:36,780
of the American contemporary fighter aircraft at the time.

147
00:10:36,781 --> 00:10:40,780
Powerful engines were only one part of the equation.

148
00:10:40,781 --> 00:10:43,380
Since the Constellation flew at high altitude,

149
00:10:43,381 --> 00:10:45,780
this required a pressurized cabin,

150
00:10:45,781 --> 00:10:48,980
one of the first available in a commercial aircraft.

151
00:10:48,981 --> 00:10:54,080
A pressurized cabin enables you to give better comfort

152
00:10:54,081 --> 00:10:56,780
to passenger and crew at higher altitudes.

153
00:10:56,781 --> 00:10:58,780
You can control temperature and pressure,

154
00:10:58,781 --> 00:11:00,580
which allows you to basically travel in your shirt sleeve

155
00:11:00,581 --> 00:11:07,620
20,000 feet where if you're unpressurized you'd be in you know minus double digit temperatures

156
00:11:07,621 --> 00:11:11,860
you'd have you need to be breathing on oxygen otherwise you'd be you'd be unconscious.

157
00:11:13,700 --> 00:11:20,500
Another interesting aspect of the Constellation design was its triple fin tail and the reason

158
00:11:20,501 --> 00:11:27,460
that was necessary is with the enormous power and wake turbulence generated by the 15-foot propeller

159
00:11:27,461 --> 00:11:35,260
arc of the 3350 engines, they needed to extend the tail wide enough to give it stability

160
00:11:35,261 --> 00:11:42,800
so they could control lift with the elevators, and the three tail fins gave it lateral stability

161
00:11:42,801 --> 00:11:47,340
so it wasn't wobbling in the air, something that you don't see on any other aircraft.

162
00:11:47,341 --> 00:11:56,060
When the first 1649 Connie came off the line, and Howard took delivery himself because he

163
00:11:56,061 --> 00:11:59,180
was able to fly it. And there are people who tell stories of him

164
00:11:59,181 --> 00:12:03,660
literally going into the cockpit, looking through the manuals and

165
00:12:03,661 --> 00:12:06,240
so forth, and then flying the airplane all by himself.

166
00:12:07,800 --> 00:12:10,840
As one of the few aviation leaders who could fly his own

167
00:12:10,841 --> 00:12:14,200
product, Hughes developed a clear vision for commercial air

168
00:12:14,201 --> 00:12:19,260
travel, larger, faster planes to maximize passenger comfort as

169
00:12:19,261 --> 00:12:23,400
well as airline profit. But just as the first constellations

170
00:12:23,401 --> 00:12:27,464
were ready. They were pressed into service in World War II.

171
00:12:27,465 --> 00:12:29,600
All available men and materials

172
00:12:29,601 --> 00:12:33,556
would be diverted toward the coming military struggle.

173
00:12:33,557 --> 00:12:36,340
Howard Hughes wanted to be part of it.

174
00:12:38,360 --> 00:12:43,260
Howard Hughes had 80 pounds of ping-pong balls stuffed into the wings and fuselage

175
00:12:43,261 --> 00:12:47,887
so that the Lockheed 14 would float if forced down at sea.

176
00:12:47,888 --> 00:12:50,280
Howard Hughes Tech will return

177
00:12:50,281 --> 00:12:51,700
on Modern Marvels.

178
00:12:53,400 --> 00:13:00,400
It might be said that modern war is a contest driven by technology.

179
00:13:00,401 --> 00:13:05,400
But even before conflicts were waged by remote control or using super computers,

180
00:13:05,401 --> 00:13:09,400
the machines of war had to employ cutting edge technology.

181
00:13:09,401 --> 00:13:16,400
Howard Hughes, who would develop helicopters, missiles and other advanced weaponry, understood this.

182
00:13:16,401 --> 00:13:22,400
In 1941, the 36-year-old invented a flexible ammunition line for fighter planes

183
00:13:22,401 --> 00:13:24,520
that would efficiently feed bullets to their guns.

184
00:13:25,520 --> 00:13:27,820
This qualified Hughes as a military supplier,

185
00:13:28,300 --> 00:13:29,900
exempting him from military service.

186
00:13:31,280 --> 00:13:32,660
Hughes was also hard of hearing,

187
00:13:33,440 --> 00:13:34,660
due to otosclerosis,

188
00:13:34,980 --> 00:13:37,240
a gradual thickening of a bone in the inner ear.

189
00:13:38,180 --> 00:13:39,560
He didn't serve in the military.

190
00:13:40,820 --> 00:13:43,440
But an ammunition line wouldn't be the only way

191
00:13:43,441 --> 00:13:45,000
Hughes would contribute to the war effort.

192
00:13:45,001 --> 00:13:54,200
German U-boats were decimating Allied transports attempting to cross the Atlantic.

193
00:13:57,500 --> 00:14:02,240
The Allies had a solution in mind, a giant flying boat,

194
00:14:03,420 --> 00:14:08,720
an aircraft that could land on water and carry needed troops and materiel.

195
00:14:08,721 --> 00:14:17,720
the answer is this giant airplane where we can fly over the North Atlantic and carry tanks and

196
00:14:17,721 --> 00:14:28,460
troops and equipment far above the German submarines in the North Atlantic aviation

197
00:14:28,461 --> 00:14:33,840
pioneer James Martin was the first to conceive of a flying boat and shared the idea with Winston

198
00:14:33,841 --> 00:14:39,600
Churchill who passed it on to FDR. Henry Kaiser, the boat builder famous for his

199
00:14:39,601 --> 00:14:44,360
Liberty ships, got on board to promote it and convinced Howard Hughes to build a

200
00:14:44,361 --> 00:14:49,880
prototype. Kaiser approached Hughes because he was an aviation hero with a

201
00:14:49,881 --> 00:14:53,880
reputation for solving engineering problems.

202
00:14:53,881 --> 00:14:57,660
The military restricted the materials that might be used to fabricate the

203
00:14:57,661 --> 00:15:02,160
experimental aircraft. This was the first puzzle facing Hughes.

204
00:15:03,340 --> 00:15:09,100
He couldn't use strategic materials like aluminum, so it handicapped him, and that's why he went to wood.

205
00:15:09,700 --> 00:15:14,077
When you build a plane out of metal, you know what each piece of metal weighs.

206
00:15:14,078 --> 00:15:15,480
There's a constant there.

207
00:15:16,020 --> 00:15:17,490
But that's not true with wood.

208
00:15:17,491 --> 00:15:20,920
Every piece of wood has a different weight than another piece of wood.

209
00:15:20,921 --> 00:15:30,800
So the complexity of building that plane, of bonding that wood to the surface and balance to it, was a monumental achievement.

210
00:15:31,340 --> 00:15:39,780
In November of 1942, Hughes and Kaiser obtained $18 million in government financing to build three flying boats.

211
00:15:40,640 --> 00:15:44,980
The design called for an aircraft capable of carrying 750 troops,

212
00:15:44,981 --> 00:15:48,420
having eight 3,000-horsepower engines

213
00:15:48,421 --> 00:15:51,740
and wings longer than any aircraft ever built,

214
00:15:52,220 --> 00:15:55,340
an impressive span of 320 feet.

215
00:15:56,620 --> 00:15:58,980
At first, it was called the HK1,

216
00:15:59,580 --> 00:16:01,800
the H for Hughes, the K for Kaiser.

217
00:16:02,260 --> 00:16:04,460
But after Kaiser dropped out of the project,

218
00:16:05,140 --> 00:16:07,060
Hughes named it the H4 Hercules.

219
00:16:07,760 --> 00:16:10,300
It later became known, much to his annoyance,

220
00:16:10,940 --> 00:16:12,240
as the Spruce Goose.

221
00:16:13,080 --> 00:16:16,180
Hughes always referred to it as my flying boat.

222
00:16:16,800 --> 00:16:20,780
It was called the Hughes Flying Boat and coined by the press as a spruce goose.

223
00:16:21,700 --> 00:16:26,680
Howard Hughes knew that the sheer size of his aircraft would require new ways for a pilot to fly it.

224
00:16:27,880 --> 00:16:32,520
That was because control surfaces such as rudders and flaps were too large and too heavy

225
00:16:32,521 --> 00:16:35,760
for a pilot to move by simple mechanical means.

226
00:16:36,420 --> 00:16:38,260
It was a huge hydraulic problem.

227
00:16:38,261 --> 00:16:40,880
To be able to move those heavy controls,

228
00:16:41,120 --> 00:16:44,360
the half the length of a football field took some technology.

229
00:16:44,960 --> 00:16:46,380
Working with his engineers,

230
00:16:47,040 --> 00:16:51,320
Hughes developed the first artificial field system for control surfaces.

231
00:16:52,340 --> 00:16:55,720
For each pound of pressure exerted on the rudder pedal in the cockpit,

232
00:16:56,240 --> 00:17:00,580
the rudder at the rear of the aircraft received 200 pounds of pressure.

233
00:17:02,060 --> 00:17:04,800
The system worked by means of hydraulic pumps.

234
00:17:05,800 --> 00:17:07,980
When the pilot moved the controls in the cockpit,

235
00:17:08,260 --> 00:17:11,100
hydraulic fluid triggered a relay valve.

236
00:17:11,101 --> 00:17:14,400
This admitted pressurized oil into a cylinder that,

237
00:17:14,401 --> 00:17:17,900
in turn, moved the control surface.

238
00:17:17,901 --> 00:17:20,900
The flying boat took three years to construct.

239
00:17:20,901 --> 00:17:25,400
World War II ended before this aviation marvel was completed.

240
00:17:25,401 --> 00:17:28,900
But in Washington, lawmakers didn't see the project

241
00:17:28,901 --> 00:17:31,500
as a triumph of aviation design.

242
00:17:31,501 --> 00:17:35,320
They saw it as a post-war white elephant and money pit.

243
00:17:35,321 --> 00:17:41,060
In August 1947, the Republican senator from Maine, Ralph Owen Brewster,

244
00:17:41,680 --> 00:17:47,580
launched an inquiry into Hughes' supposed misappropriation of funds to build a useless aircraft.

245
00:17:48,580 --> 00:17:53,620
It was an effort to discredit Hughes as a pilot and an aircraft manufacturer,

246
00:17:53,940 --> 00:17:59,920
and Hughes took it seriously, and he grabbed an armful of manila folders and took on the Senate.

247
00:18:01,040 --> 00:18:05,000
Senator Brewster's story, as related here yesterday, is a pack of lies.

248
00:18:05,320 --> 00:18:08,240
and I can tear it apart if allowed to cross-question him.

249
00:18:08,500 --> 00:18:11,040
As you'll see in some of the Senate hearing clips,

250
00:18:11,280 --> 00:18:12,500
that he was amazing.

251
00:18:12,980 --> 00:18:15,340
I put the sweat of my life into this thing.

252
00:18:15,420 --> 00:18:17,420
I have my reputation rolled up in it,

253
00:18:17,780 --> 00:18:20,300
and I have stated several times that if it's a failure,

254
00:18:20,420 --> 00:18:23,300
I'll probably leave this country and never come back, and I mean it.

255
00:18:23,820 --> 00:18:25,260
I don't think he would have said that

256
00:18:25,261 --> 00:18:27,580
unless he felt in his gut that it was airworthy.

257
00:18:28,940 --> 00:18:31,300
The government cleared Hughes of any wrongdoing,

258
00:18:31,600 --> 00:18:33,680
but he still wanted to prove to the world

259
00:18:33,681 --> 00:18:35,440
that the flying boat was worthwhile.

260
00:18:36,400 --> 00:18:38,740
He had it moved to a launch site in Long Beach Harbor

261
00:18:38,741 --> 00:18:39,680
near Los Angeles.

262
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,840
This required transporting it in pieces by truck

263
00:18:43,841 --> 00:18:47,460
for 26 miles over a period of two days.

264
00:18:51,480 --> 00:18:54,580
Then on Sunday, November 2, 1947,

265
00:18:55,460 --> 00:18:57,000
Hughes took a seat at the controls.

266
00:18:59,740 --> 00:19:02,460
He told the media that he would taxi the flying boat

267
00:19:02,461 --> 00:19:04,600
in Long Beach Harbor for a demonstration run.

268
00:19:05,700 --> 00:19:07,840
But then Hughes did something that surprised

269
00:19:07,841 --> 00:19:09,540
even the crew aboard the aircraft.

270
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,840
It must have felt good after two high-speed taxis

271
00:19:14,841 --> 00:19:17,560
because he taxied back, kicked all the press off,

272
00:19:17,620 --> 00:19:20,140
except for one fellow that had the latest recording equipment.

273
00:19:20,840 --> 00:19:25,140
And when the co-pilot said he asked for 90, 15-degree flaps,

274
00:19:25,220 --> 00:19:28,160
he knew it was going up because it was wanting to leave the ground at 90,

275
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:29,140
and sure enough, it did.

276
00:19:29,141 --> 00:19:35,880
For throttle, it's 60, it bounces to 65, it's 70, it's 75.

277
00:19:37,040 --> 00:19:38,440
And I believe we are airborne.

278
00:19:39,240 --> 00:19:40,860
We are airborne, ladies and gentlemen.

279
00:19:41,320 --> 00:19:42,820
We were really up in the air.

280
00:19:43,120 --> 00:19:45,780
The guy interviewing him during the flight,

281
00:19:45,880 --> 00:19:48,840
the one person that was on board with a wire recorder,

282
00:19:49,360 --> 00:19:53,200
and said, Mr. Hughes, were you surprised when the airplane took off the water?

283
00:19:53,560 --> 00:19:54,800
Howard, did you expect that?

284
00:19:55,440 --> 00:19:57,100
Certainly, I'd like to make surprises.

285
00:19:57,101 --> 00:20:00,100
And Howard answered, I like to make surprises.

286
00:20:01,280 --> 00:20:04,740
Howard Hughes flew the flying boat to an altitude of 70 feet

287
00:20:04,741 --> 00:20:08,840
and a distance of about one mile across the waters of Long Beach Harbor.

288
00:20:09,680 --> 00:20:11,280
It never flew again.

289
00:20:12,240 --> 00:20:14,880
But Hughes considered it one of his greatest accomplishments.

290
00:20:16,740 --> 00:20:19,580
The flying boat was as close to Hughes' heart

291
00:20:19,581 --> 00:20:22,880
as any single enterprise he ever undertook.

292
00:20:23,280 --> 00:20:24,780
When he really wanted to impress somebody,

293
00:20:24,781 --> 00:20:30,360
he would give them a tour of the flying boat. Hughes kept his flying boat flight

294
00:20:30,361 --> 00:20:35,880
ready in a hangar for decades. I think this is the idiosyncratic Mr. Hughes.

295
00:20:35,881 --> 00:20:42,300
They would periodically turn over the engines to make sure they were okay. In

296
00:20:42,301 --> 00:20:48,540
1982 the flying boat was put on exhibit in Long Beach and in 1995 was moved to a

297
00:20:48,541 --> 00:20:52,520
Museum in Oregon. Built of wood to solve a wartime

298
00:20:52,521 --> 00:20:57,540
materials crisis that passed, the flying boat was never put into regular production.

299
00:20:58,540 --> 00:21:02,540
But the artificial field system Hughes pioneered remains in use today.

300
00:21:03,540 --> 00:21:11,540
The flying boat still holds the record as the biggest wooden seaplane ever built, and it was a forerunner to aircraft such as the C-130.

301
00:21:11,541 --> 00:21:18,000
Although many thought the flying boat would sour Hughes' relations

302
00:21:18,001 --> 00:21:21,500
with the military forever, in the coming decade,

303
00:21:21,501 --> 00:21:24,580
a sweet reward was on its way for Howard Hughes.

304
00:21:24,581 --> 00:21:29,540
Military historians believe that Hughes' H-1 racer design

305
00:21:29,541 --> 00:21:32,540
was adapted by the Japanese during World War II

306
00:21:32,541 --> 00:21:35,540
to create the famed Zero Warplane.

307
00:21:35,541 --> 00:21:39,540
Howard Hughes Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

308
00:21:41,540 --> 00:21:44,440
As World War II transitioned to the Cold War,

309
00:21:44,960 --> 00:21:48,800
Howard Hughes believed military contracts would be profit centers for his companies.

310
00:21:50,380 --> 00:21:54,760
Accordingly, he pushed forward with a design for a military photo reconnaissance aircraft,

311
00:21:55,360 --> 00:21:56,460
called the XF-11.

312
00:21:57,160 --> 00:21:59,500
It was a very efficient airfoil.

313
00:21:59,660 --> 00:22:02,640
It was expected to fly up over 40,000 feet,

314
00:22:02,641 --> 00:22:05,640
which was very high altitude in the 1940s,

315
00:22:06,240 --> 00:22:08,760
and even have a range up to 5,000 miles.

316
00:22:09,400 --> 00:22:16,640
Although some military historians suggest that Hughes' XF-11 is a forerunner of the famed U-2 spy plane of the 1960s,

317
00:22:17,900 --> 00:22:22,420
structurally, it's closer to the twin-boom World War II fighter, the P-38.

318
00:22:24,340 --> 00:22:28,280
A significant feature of Hughes' aircraft was its counter-rotating props,

319
00:22:29,080 --> 00:22:33,660
a feature that he insisted upon because it would add power, and therefore speed.

320
00:22:34,580 --> 00:22:38,860
Double propellers, one goes clockwise and one goes counterclockwise,

321
00:22:39,040 --> 00:22:42,460
so you get a little extra thrust by doing that.

322
00:22:42,520 --> 00:22:46,180
Again, a very revolutionary idea in that time period.

323
00:22:46,900 --> 00:22:51,540
On Sunday, July 7, 1946, when Hughes flew the prototype,

324
00:22:52,440 --> 00:22:55,140
it would be a flight he would remember for the rest of his life.

325
00:22:59,160 --> 00:23:01,460
He was anxious to get the plane into the air

326
00:23:01,461 --> 00:23:05,540
and neglected to take standard safety precautions.

327
00:23:05,640 --> 00:23:08,940
He was supposed to have 600 gallons of fuel on board.

328
00:23:09,040 --> 00:23:11,340
He put 1,200 gallons of fuel on board.

329
00:23:11,440 --> 00:23:13,400
He was supposed to have a chase aircraft

330
00:23:13,500 --> 00:23:15,400
on a similar frequency.

331
00:23:15,500 --> 00:23:17,940
They were on different frequencies.

332
00:23:18,040 --> 00:23:20,440
About an hour and 15 minutes into the flight,

333
00:23:20,540 --> 00:23:22,840
Hughes noticed something wrong.

334
00:23:22,940 --> 00:23:25,880
One of the two props on the right side had gone into reverse.

335
00:23:28,060 --> 00:23:30,820
The XF-11 began to pull right as Hughes

336
00:23:30,821 --> 00:23:35,420
as Hughes fought to turn the plane to the left.

337
00:23:35,421 --> 00:23:39,320
So now he had one propeller in high pitch giving him thrust,

338
00:23:39,321 --> 00:23:42,760
and one in reverse, just like you experience when you land

339
00:23:42,761 --> 00:23:44,640
in an airliner and they go into reverse,

340
00:23:44,641 --> 00:23:48,980
and we all hear the noise and the effect of that.

341
00:23:48,981 --> 00:23:51,040
When it smashed into a Beverly Hills home

342
00:23:51,041 --> 00:23:54,440
and burst into flames, the XF-11 was traveling

343
00:23:54,441 --> 00:23:57,820
at 155 miles per hour.

344
00:23:57,821 --> 00:24:00,520
Hughes was badly injured.

345
00:24:00,521 --> 00:24:05,380
He severely cracked his skull, broke all the ribs on his heart side,

346
00:24:05,500 --> 00:24:07,840
dislodged his heart, broke his legs, some burns.

347
00:24:08,780 --> 00:24:10,100
But he survived.

348
00:24:11,860 --> 00:24:15,820
During his recovery in the hospital, a period of a month and a half,

349
00:24:16,580 --> 00:24:18,260
Hughes was given morphine for pain.

350
00:24:19,120 --> 00:24:22,620
And later, doctors allowed Hughes to give himself injections of codeine,

351
00:24:23,060 --> 00:24:26,220
a derivation of morphine that doctors believed to be less addictive.

352
00:24:26,221 --> 00:24:31,780
While he was in the hospital, he designed the modern-day hospital bed.

353
00:24:32,400 --> 00:24:37,200
He had engineers design servos so that the bed he could turn because of his burns.

354
00:24:37,340 --> 00:24:39,740
It was pretty interesting. His mind never really stopped.

355
00:24:40,620 --> 00:24:43,380
While recuperating and tinkering with his hospital bed,

356
00:24:44,420 --> 00:24:46,880
Hughes spoke with Glenn Oedekirk, his chief engineer.

357
00:24:48,160 --> 00:24:52,420
Together they realized that the counter-rotating props were the flaw in the XF-11.

358
00:24:52,421 --> 00:24:57,820
Hughes ordered a second prototype of the aircraft built without them.

359
00:24:57,821 --> 00:25:03,800
Although he kept up the appearance of being in command, and later successfully flew the

360
00:25:03,801 --> 00:25:11,080
second prototype, the aviator who emerged from the hospital on August 12, 1946, was a vastly

361
00:25:11,081 --> 00:25:12,232
different man.

362
00:25:12,233 --> 00:25:19,060
He had a secret, and that was that he was going to continue taking these medicines.

363
00:25:19,061 --> 00:25:27,840
And I think what happened is he kind of had the makings of being an obsessive-compulsive disorder personality,

364
00:25:28,120 --> 00:25:32,040
but the medicines amplified that even more.

365
00:25:32,980 --> 00:25:40,500
Obsessive-compulsive disorder, or OCD, is a form of mental illness that causes sufferers to engage in repetitive acts

366
00:25:40,501 --> 00:25:45,300
and to be consumed with irrational fears, in Hugh's case, to fear germs.

367
00:25:46,260 --> 00:25:49,020
One thing everybody needs to understand is, at the time,

368
00:25:49,180 --> 00:25:51,840
obsessive-compulsive disorder had not been diagnosed.

369
00:25:52,560 --> 00:25:54,040
Nobody knew what the heck it was.

370
00:25:55,280 --> 00:25:59,900
The eccentric millionaire demanded that his aides use Kleenex to hand him any object.

371
00:26:00,700 --> 00:26:05,040
He wants to use a Kleenex to hold against everything so he doesn't touch it.

372
00:26:05,240 --> 00:26:09,320
He doesn't want to touch a doorknob because he's worried about who touched it before.

373
00:26:10,120 --> 00:26:14,340
The U.S. Air Force canceled the XF-11 program in a cost-cutting measure.

374
00:26:14,341 --> 00:26:16,340
The decision left Hughes frustrated.

375
00:26:16,341 --> 00:26:19,340
He thought the aircraft was ahead of its time.

376
00:26:19,341 --> 00:26:24,340
But his genius for design aside, the U.S. military reportedly

377
00:26:24,341 --> 00:26:27,340
found Hughes difficult to work with on a personal level.

378
00:26:27,341 --> 00:26:31,340
Howard was kind of of the thinking that if you design

379
00:26:31,341 --> 00:26:34,340
the fastest plane in the world, the military would be knocking

380
00:26:34,341 --> 00:26:36,340
on your door and want to buy them.

381
00:26:36,341 --> 00:26:38,340
And that's not the way the military does it.

382
00:26:38,341 --> 00:26:42,340
They determine specifications, and they go out and they compete

383
00:26:42,341 --> 00:26:44,640
and they compete these specifications.

384
00:26:44,641 --> 00:26:48,440
And you make a bid, and then you're responsible to do

385
00:26:48,441 --> 00:26:51,140
exactly what you said you'd do in the contract.

386
00:26:51,141 --> 00:26:53,320
And that's not the way Howard operated.

387
00:26:53,321 --> 00:26:54,580
He was his own man.

388
00:26:54,581 --> 00:26:57,680
He did it the way he wanted to do it.

389
00:26:57,681 --> 00:27:00,620
Despite this, Hughes was, as always,

390
00:27:00,621 --> 00:27:02,860
determined to obtain more military contracts

391
00:27:02,861 --> 00:27:05,060
for his companies.

392
00:27:05,061 --> 00:27:10,140
He saw a future in something called electronics.

393
00:27:10,141 --> 00:27:17,740
He was working with some of his people to find an electronic hearing aid that would improve his hearing.

394
00:27:18,780 --> 00:27:21,800
And though the word electronics was hardly even known,

395
00:27:22,360 --> 00:27:27,160
he recognized that electronics somehow might be able to provide the answer.

396
00:27:27,720 --> 00:27:35,120
In late 1948, electronically enhanced target tracking was high on the list of United States Air Force projects.

397
00:27:35,121 --> 00:27:40,760
military planners wanted equipment that allowed fighter planes to destroy enemy aircraft in any

398
00:27:40,761 --> 00:27:42,325
weather, day or night.

399
00:27:42,326 --> 00:27:47,660
Engineers at Hughes Aircraft have been working on a guidance system project

400
00:27:47,661 --> 00:27:51,904
for months. Being ahead of the curve paid off.

401
00:27:51,905 --> 00:27:56,240
The Air Force awarded an $8 million contract to

402
00:27:56,241 --> 00:28:03,040
Hughes Aircraft to build and install Falcon missile weapon systems in 200 Lockheed F-94 fighters.

403
00:28:05,120 --> 00:28:13,640
The Falcon missile was an Air Force project that was tended to be launched at enemy bombers.

404
00:28:14,440 --> 00:28:22,860
The pilot would find the target on his own radar, and then he would tell the missile, in effect, where to look.

405
00:28:23,980 --> 00:28:29,040
In 1951, Hughes built a missile manufacturing facility in Arizona.

406
00:28:29,041 --> 00:28:34,220
There were 15,000 employees, 1,000 of whom were top engineers.

407
00:28:35,120 --> 00:28:46,460
By the 1960s, Hughes was developing the next-generation Phoenix missile, fitted to Navy F-14 Tomcat jets, that used a radar-based guidance system similar to the Falcon.

408
00:28:48,840 --> 00:28:52,640
Later, the Maverick missile system represented another step.

409
00:28:53,260 --> 00:28:57,840
It was laser-guided and allowed the pilot to see the target remotely on a television screen.

410
00:28:59,060 --> 00:29:02,000
Today, we call these weapons smart bombs.

411
00:29:02,001 --> 00:29:09,000
By the end of the 60s, driven by growing government orders, Howard Hughes would employ 80,000 workers.

412
00:29:09,001 --> 00:29:18,000
Finally, Hughes was a military mogul, but his advancing mental illness forced him to withdraw from day-to-day operations.

413
00:29:18,001 --> 00:29:26,000
It would be up to his managers to take his namesake companies to the next level, into orbit.

414
00:29:26,001 --> 00:29:30,840
into orbit. Hughes Phoenix missile, one of the company's

415
00:29:30,841 --> 00:29:34,840
first successful military contracts, was retired in October

416
00:29:34,841 --> 00:29:40,920
2004 after 30 years of service. Howard Hughes Tech will return

417
00:29:40,921 --> 00:29:43,880
on Modern Marvels.

418
00:29:47,880 --> 00:29:52,040
Howard Hughes, high speed pilot, developer of the flying boat,

419
00:29:52,041 --> 00:29:53,260
and technology visionary,

420
00:29:53,740 --> 00:29:56,920
and by the 1960s developed another valuable skill,

421
00:29:57,720 --> 00:30:00,020
recruiting talented engineers and managers.

422
00:30:00,940 --> 00:30:03,260
This was crucial to his corporate survival

423
00:30:03,261 --> 00:30:05,920
because he'd ventured into engineering territory

424
00:30:05,921 --> 00:30:08,800
far beyond his expertise as a stick-and-rutter pilot.

425
00:30:10,080 --> 00:30:11,540
He was a hands-on guy

426
00:30:11,541 --> 00:30:13,360
when he was building the first racing planes

427
00:30:13,361 --> 00:30:16,140
because these essentially were glorified garages

428
00:30:16,141 --> 00:30:18,100
where a handful of people were doing the work

429
00:30:18,101 --> 00:30:19,860
and they were all working together and so forth.

430
00:30:20,040 --> 00:30:21,560
But later on, what he liked to do

431
00:30:21,561 --> 00:30:25,740
was bring into his operations talented people, good people,

432
00:30:26,480 --> 00:30:29,180
people who were willing to take a risk in some areas,

433
00:30:29,280 --> 00:30:30,120
who wanted to experiment,

434
00:30:30,320 --> 00:30:33,140
because working for Hughes had a certain allure about it,

435
00:30:33,300 --> 00:30:34,240
a certain glamour.

436
00:30:34,500 --> 00:30:36,340
Here was this guy who'd set all of these records.

437
00:30:37,080 --> 00:30:38,740
He was this mythic person,

438
00:30:38,820 --> 00:30:40,860
even before he was very old in this world.

439
00:30:41,540 --> 00:30:43,500
By the early 1950s,

440
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:46,360
Hughes' talented engineers had made the transition

441
00:30:46,361 --> 00:30:48,760
from fixed-wing aircraft to helicopters.

442
00:30:49,620 --> 00:30:51,320
Their first effort was a behemoth,

443
00:30:51,560 --> 00:30:55,220
known as the Flying Crane.

444
00:30:55,221 --> 00:30:59,060
The XH-17, which had a two-blade main rotor system

445
00:30:59,061 --> 00:31:03,020
with a diameter of 134 feet, was capable of flying

446
00:31:03,021 --> 00:31:06,020
at a gross weight of more than 50,000 pounds.

447
00:31:08,800 --> 00:31:11,020
The prototype didn't attract a production deal

448
00:31:11,021 --> 00:31:13,600
from the military, but it was the beginning

449
00:31:13,601 --> 00:31:17,020
of a productive time for Hughes Helicopter Division.

450
00:31:17,021 --> 00:31:21,520
Its engineers decided to go from large to small helicopters.

451
00:31:21,521 --> 00:31:27,900
By 1961, Hughes Helicopter Division was manufacturing a training and reconnaissance helicopter,

452
00:31:28,860 --> 00:31:32,120
the Model 269, for civilian and military use.

453
00:31:33,060 --> 00:31:35,800
It's maneuverable. You can see where you're going.

454
00:31:36,680 --> 00:31:39,080
It's very responsive to controls.

455
00:31:40,780 --> 00:31:45,740
Hughes engineers modified the tail rotor, making it smaller and therefore quieter.

456
00:31:45,741 --> 00:31:52,180
In this and later models, they also addressed an issue of pilot and passenger safety in

457
00:31:52,181 --> 00:31:56,120
the event of a crash by creating an energy-absorbing structure.

458
00:31:56,121 --> 00:32:03,580
This is designed so that the tubular structure and those beams across underneath will collapse

459
00:32:03,581 --> 00:32:10,120
first to absorb some energy, then the bottom of the fuselage will collapse, and finally

460
00:32:10,121 --> 00:32:14,620
the seat structure will collapse, and the combination of those three gives the pilot

461
00:32:14,621 --> 00:32:17,520
a great deal of protection.

462
00:32:17,521 --> 00:32:22,120
The engineering decisions made by Hughes Group were considered so sound, designers

463
00:32:22,121 --> 00:32:25,600
continued to use them as helicopter technology advanced.

464
00:32:25,601 --> 00:32:32,240
The seat collapsing concept started with this helicopter.

465
00:32:32,241 --> 00:32:40,540
It was carried on into the 086 Cayuse and then into the age 64 Apache.

466
00:32:40,541 --> 00:32:43,840
And it's served a purpose very well in all of those.

467
00:32:43,841 --> 00:32:48,440
And by building on one another, we've been able to take account of the best features of each,

468
00:32:49,340 --> 00:32:51,100
keep getting a better and better helicopter.

469
00:32:53,240 --> 00:32:57,120
The 1960s also saw Hughes engineers shooting for the stars.

470
00:32:57,560 --> 00:33:05,080
They created, and in 1963, launched Syncom, the first geosynchronous communication satellite,

471
00:33:05,500 --> 00:33:08,320
enabling around the world live TV broadcasts.

472
00:33:08,321 --> 00:33:15,320
The conventional wisdom at the time is that satellites were impractical.

473
00:33:15,321 --> 00:33:22,320
They were too heavy to be launched and too complicated to last long enough to be commercially attractive.

474
00:33:22,321 --> 00:33:24,320
That was the challenge.

475
00:33:24,321 --> 00:33:29,320
A geosynchronous satellite maintains an unchanging position over the planet,

476
00:33:29,321 --> 00:33:33,320
synchronizing each orbit with the revolution of the Earth.

477
00:33:33,321 --> 00:33:38,040
So if you were sitting on the ground looking up at the satellite,

478
00:33:38,041 --> 00:33:41,760
it would appear to you always to be in the same spot.

479
00:33:42,860 --> 00:33:45,540
Syncom stabilized itself by rotating continuously,

480
00:33:46,740 --> 00:33:50,400
driven by small thrusters positioned along its cylindrical shape.

481
00:33:51,080 --> 00:33:55,560
By spinning this cylinder, it acted like a gyroscope.

482
00:33:56,380 --> 00:33:59,360
So in effect, it stabilized itself.

483
00:33:59,361 --> 00:34:03,360
In orbit 22,000 miles above the Earth,

484
00:34:03,361 --> 00:34:06,360
Syncom was in effect a relay.

485
00:34:06,361 --> 00:34:10,360
It received a signal from Earth, which it amplified

486
00:34:10,361 --> 00:34:15,360
and then transmitted to a terrestrial receiver.

487
00:34:15,361 --> 00:34:18,360
Hughes satellites were later capable of transmitting thousands

488
00:34:18,361 --> 00:34:21,360
of simultaneous telephone conversations

489
00:34:21,361 --> 00:34:25,360
and hundreds of simultaneous color telecasts.

490
00:34:25,361 --> 00:34:29,360
But Hughes engineers had more innovations to bring to satellites.

491
00:34:29,361 --> 00:34:30,784
satellite technology.

492
00:34:30,785 --> 00:34:35,600
They created the ATS-1, the first satellite capable of taking a picture

493
00:34:35,601 --> 00:34:39,120
of an entire hemisphere of the Earth as viewed from space.

494
00:34:39,121 --> 00:34:47,500
Since we were using a spinning satellite, if we put a camera in the satellite, then

495
00:34:47,501 --> 00:34:53,080
after a certain number of revolutions, it would scan one strip, scan another strip,

496
00:34:53,081 --> 00:34:55,257
scan another strip, scan another strip.

497
00:34:55,258 --> 00:34:58,160
Pretty soon you had a complete picture of the Earth.

498
00:34:58,161 --> 00:35:04,880
Meteorologists soon realized that the images transmitted from the new satellite would allow

499
00:35:04,881 --> 00:35:12,660
them for the first time to keep real-time watch on hurricanes and other storm systems.

500
00:35:12,661 --> 00:35:15,680
Hughes found great success with his satellite division.

501
00:35:15,681 --> 00:35:20,560
His engineers developed what would eventually become known commercially as DirecTV, the

502
00:35:20,561 --> 00:35:25,960
first delivery of a television signal to a compact home satellite dish.

503
00:35:25,961 --> 00:35:35,734
It must have taken about 30 years of evolution before the satellites reached a point where they could have enough radiated power to permit the reception of television

504
00:35:35,735 --> 00:35:39,620
and small dishes that we have today, 18-inch dishes on the ground.

505
00:35:39,621 --> 00:35:43,500
I think it only took about two years to build the satellite itself.

506
00:35:43,940 --> 00:35:46,410
There are 20 million of them in the United States right now.

507
00:35:46,411 --> 00:35:49,580
There are 20 million homes that have them. Some of them have multiple dishes.

508
00:35:49,581 --> 00:35:54,460
The engineers Hughes put in place were making great technological strides,

509
00:35:54,880 --> 00:35:57,668
but Hughes watched their triumphs from afar.

510
00:35:57,669 --> 00:36:00,900
His increasingly precarious mental state pushed him

511
00:36:00,901 --> 00:36:02,570
deeper into isolation.

512
00:36:02,571 --> 00:36:07,960
In the fall of 1968, Hughes further cut himself off from human contact,

513
00:36:08,860 --> 00:36:11,960
moving into a Las Vegas hotel suite with blacked out windows.

514
00:36:11,961 --> 00:36:20,300
while in seclusion he went on a 100 million dollar buying spree acquiring casinos local

515
00:36:20,301 --> 00:36:25,940
airports and a television station and thus became the silver state's biggest landlord

516
00:36:25,941 --> 00:36:32,060
his net worth increased in other ways because while antitrust regulations had forced Hughes

517
00:36:32,061 --> 00:36:41,060
to sell his controlling interest in TWA in 1966 the sale created a windfall for him after brokers

518
00:36:41,061 --> 00:36:45,006
fees, Hughes received a check for half a billion dollars.

519
00:36:45,007 --> 00:36:47,360
He added this to after-tax profits

520
00:36:47,361 --> 00:36:54,680
produced by his oil equipment business, as much as $20 million annually. In April of

521
00:36:54,681 --> 00:37:00,283
1968, Fortune magazine declared Howard Hughes worth $1.3 billion.

522
00:37:00,284 --> 00:37:02,180
He was the richest man

523
00:37:02,181 --> 00:37:02,932
in America.

524
00:37:02,933 --> 00:37:08,400
But while his technological empire and wealth would expand greatly, his personal

525
00:37:08,401 --> 00:37:12,300
world would dramatically diminish.

526
00:37:12,301 --> 00:37:16,320
More than 50% of geosynchronous satellites in orbit today were

527
00:37:16,321 --> 00:37:20,020
developed by Hughes companies.

528
00:37:20,021 --> 00:37:23,080
Howard Hughes Tech will return on Modern Marvels.

529
00:37:27,800 --> 00:37:32,920
July 20, 1969, a triumph for American technical know-how

530
00:37:32,921 --> 00:37:35,680
as Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin walked on the moon.

531
00:37:35,681 --> 00:37:38,020
One giant leap for mankind.

532
00:37:38,021 --> 00:37:40,440
Howard Hughes played a role in this history-making moment,

533
00:37:40,960 --> 00:37:44,500
because three years earlier, the Hughes-designed Surveyor 1

534
00:37:44,501 --> 00:37:49,480
became the first craft to soft land on the moon on June 1, 1966.

535
00:37:51,620 --> 00:37:55,020
Thirty-six minutes after settling down in the ocean of storms,

536
00:37:56,100 --> 00:37:59,700
the Hughes craft sent back the first televised photographs of the surface.

537
00:38:01,140 --> 00:38:07,020
I remember being in the control room at JPL, Jet Propulsion Lab,

538
00:38:07,021 --> 00:38:10,580
when we made the first landing with Surveyor.

539
00:38:10,980 --> 00:38:13,260
And as a television picture developed,

540
00:38:14,300 --> 00:38:18,780
there was the landing leg of the Surveyor, there was the pad.

541
00:38:19,420 --> 00:38:23,620
I will never forget that because it was the first image

542
00:38:23,621 --> 00:38:30,860
that anyone had ever seen of the surface of another body in space.

543
00:38:32,140 --> 00:38:35,300
Retro rockets that applied the right amount of reverse thrust

544
00:38:35,301 --> 00:38:37,900
were the key to the successful landing of the Hughes craft.

545
00:38:39,040 --> 00:38:44,680
The retro rockets were controlled by, in effect, a radar signal

546
00:38:44,681 --> 00:38:51,480
that they generated by bouncing a radar signal off the surface of the moon

547
00:38:51,481 --> 00:38:52,520
and then receiving it.

548
00:38:52,900 --> 00:38:55,680
That way it could tell how far away the moon was.

549
00:38:56,460 --> 00:38:59,280
Later surveyor missions transmitted thousands of photographs

550
00:38:59,281 --> 00:39:02,420
and conducted chemical analyses of the lunar soil,

551
00:39:02,900 --> 00:39:04,860
preparing for manned landings to come.

552
00:39:05,300 --> 00:39:12,180
Some Hughes biographers speculate that he watched the lunar landing on TV from his dark

553
00:39:12,181 --> 00:39:15,860
Las Vegas hotel room.

554
00:39:15,861 --> 00:39:21,480
He lived, many believed, in a world of pain, drug addiction and compulsive behavior.

555
00:39:21,481 --> 00:39:26,340
He's evidencing signs of OCD in the foods he wants to eat.

556
00:39:26,341 --> 00:39:28,980
He has a standard meal that he orders.

557
00:39:28,981 --> 00:39:34,620
For Howard Hughes, the standard meal was steak, mashed potatoes and green peas.

558
00:39:34,621 --> 00:39:36,160
and a glass of milk.

559
00:39:37,000 --> 00:39:39,360
It would be downright bizarre when it came to the dessert

560
00:39:39,361 --> 00:39:42,840
because he liked chocolate cake, but he used a ruler.

561
00:39:43,280 --> 00:39:46,020
It had to be, you know, the edges of the chocolate cake

562
00:39:46,021 --> 00:39:49,500
had to be perfectly straight, the way the cake was cut.

563
00:39:49,780 --> 00:39:52,440
And if it wasn't, it was sent back to the kitchen.

564
00:39:54,200 --> 00:39:57,500
As Hughes continued to struggle with his addiction and mental illness,

565
00:39:58,060 --> 00:40:01,780
his far-reaching empire continued to produce engineering marvels.

566
00:40:01,781 --> 00:40:09,720
By the early 1970s, his helicopter, aircraft, satellite and electronics divisions were primary

567
00:40:09,721 --> 00:40:12,020
military suppliers.

568
00:40:12,021 --> 00:40:16,380
That made his companies a good contact for the CIA.

569
00:40:16,381 --> 00:40:22,360
The spy agency was aware that in April of 1968, a Soviet submarine had been lost and

570
00:40:22,361 --> 00:40:25,060
it wanted to recover the vessel.

571
00:40:25,061 --> 00:40:30,720
To accomplish the task, the CIA turned to one of America's most secretive businessmen,

572
00:40:30,721 --> 00:40:31,720
Howard Hughes.

573
00:40:31,721 --> 00:40:38,340
The appeal of the Hughes operations was the great secrecy that shrouded almost all of

574
00:40:38,341 --> 00:40:39,340
Hughes' operations.

575
00:40:39,341 --> 00:40:44,400
He was a private businessman, virtually all his companies were private, answerable to

576
00:40:44,401 --> 00:40:45,400
no stockholders.

577
00:40:45,401 --> 00:40:51,020
And here was an American corporation that could keep a secret.

578
00:40:51,021 --> 00:40:55,120
The Soviet submarine was carrying nuclear torpedoes and coating equipment.

579
00:40:55,121 --> 00:41:00,700
When hydrogen gas built up inside it and exploded, it sank 17,000 feet to the bottom of the

580
00:41:00,701 --> 00:41:01,600
of the ocean.

581
00:41:01,601 --> 00:41:06,860
The CIA wanted to build a specialized mining ship to recover the Soviet sub.

582
00:41:08,940 --> 00:41:16,940
At 618 feet 8 inches, the Hughes Mining Barge, or HMV-1, was longer than two football fields.

583
00:41:17,500 --> 00:41:21,517
A steel A-frame rose from its deck to a height of 200 feet.

584
00:41:21,518 --> 00:41:23,900
At the center, there was an opening

585
00:41:23,901 --> 00:41:26,212
providing hidden access to the ocean.

586
00:41:26,213 --> 00:41:29,900
And through this opening, an onboard derrick was capable of

587
00:41:29,901 --> 00:41:34,260
of lifting objects weighing as much as 14 million pounds

588
00:41:34,261 --> 00:41:36,920
or reeling out more than three miles of pipe

589
00:41:36,921 --> 00:41:40,280
into the depths of the sea.

590
00:41:40,281 --> 00:41:44,280
HMB-1 was a mining barge in name only.

591
00:41:44,281 --> 00:41:47,920
The vessel, also known as the Hughes Glomar Explorer,

592
00:41:47,921 --> 00:41:50,020
had room for the entire Soviet submarine

593
00:41:50,021 --> 00:41:53,640
to be brought up inside.

594
00:41:53,641 --> 00:41:56,920
The salvage operation was only partially successful,

595
00:41:56,921 --> 00:42:01,520
as the sub broke in half as it was recovered.

596
00:42:01,521 --> 00:42:05,720
But the CIA was able to examine two Soviet nuclear torpedoes

597
00:42:05,721 --> 00:42:08,320
and coating equipment.

598
00:42:08,321 --> 00:42:10,420
A burial at sea was performed for the recovered

599
00:42:10,421 --> 00:42:12,160
remains of six Soviet crewmen.

600
00:42:16,300 --> 00:42:19,840
In 1975, a few years after the Glomar Explorer mission

601
00:42:19,841 --> 00:42:24,620
was complete, Howard Hughes was 70 years old.

602
00:42:24,621 --> 00:42:28,260
He had not been photographed in public since 1952,

603
00:42:28,261 --> 00:42:31,680
and had lived in a succession of darkened hotel suites,

604
00:42:31,681 --> 00:42:36,780
from Las Vegas to the Bahamas to London to Mexico.

605
00:42:36,781 --> 00:42:39,540
His companies were run by an army of managers,

606
00:42:39,541 --> 00:42:42,880
most of whom had never met their boss.

607
00:42:42,881 --> 00:42:46,000
As he wasted away in a world of perpetual darkness,

608
00:42:46,001 --> 00:42:49,040
Hughes had drifted far from the beloved dashing aviation

609
00:42:49,041 --> 00:42:52,040
hero of his early years.

610
00:42:52,041 --> 00:42:55,300
Now a shadowy enigma mired in mental illness and addiction,

611
00:42:56,040 --> 00:43:00,700
he was daily ingesting massive amounts of Valium and injecting himself with codeine.

612
00:43:01,560 --> 00:43:06,140
In his final days, he stopped eating and weighed barely more than 100 pounds.

613
00:43:06,760 --> 00:43:09,840
Alarmed, his doctors put him on a plane bound for a Houston hospital.

614
00:43:10,400 --> 00:43:16,020
He died en route on April 5, 1976 at 1.27 p.m.

615
00:43:16,021 --> 00:43:23,020
He left behind an enormous legacy in aviation and technology.

616
00:43:23,021 --> 00:43:27,020
He set records around test tracks and around the globe.

617
00:43:27,021 --> 00:43:29,020
He proved that the world's largest wooden aircraft

618
00:43:29,021 --> 00:43:31,020
was flight-worthy.

619
00:43:31,021 --> 00:43:34,020
Hughes and his engineers found the fresh air of innovation

620
00:43:34,021 --> 00:43:37,020
when others weren't able to get off the ground.

621
00:43:37,021 --> 00:43:40,020
Since Hughes died, the companies he founded have continued

622
00:43:40,021 --> 00:43:43,020
to do remarkable work, making a space probe

623
00:43:43,021 --> 00:43:45,860
a space probe that mapped Venus by radar,

624
00:43:45,861 --> 00:43:48,860
and designing Galileo, the first spacecraft

625
00:43:48,861 --> 00:43:52,320
to penetrate Jupiter's atmosphere.

626
00:43:52,321 --> 00:43:54,260
A medical institute that Hughes founded

627
00:43:54,261 --> 00:43:58,560
conducts research today in nanotechnology, stem cells,

628
00:43:58,561 --> 00:44:02,380
and seeks a cure for obsessive compulsive disorder.

629
00:44:02,381 --> 00:44:04,900
Whenever we board a commercial airliner,

630
00:44:04,901 --> 00:44:07,820
watch television via satellite, or marvel

631
00:44:07,821 --> 00:44:11,120
at America's military might, we would do well

632
00:44:11,121 --> 00:44:13,960
remember the man who died in torment.

633
00:44:13,961 --> 00:44:18,760
He flew faster than his peers, took risks that others avoided,

634
00:44:18,761 --> 00:44:22,460
and was at heart an aviator dedicated to the science

635
00:44:22,461 --> 00:44:25,120
and the art of innovation.

